course: dictionary
lesson: 14400
title: Outright and what it tells you
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Outright is a word with two jobs. As an adjective it describes a noun, like «an outright winner». As an adverb it tells you how something was done, like «She told him outright». Look at the words around it to find which job it does.
explain: Outright means complete, total, and with no doubt. «The race was an outright win for Maya» means she won clearly, with nobody close behind. If you swap in the word total, the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Outright can also mean openly and directly, with nothing hidden. «He told me outright that he did not want to come» shows he said it straight out. It can also mean paying in full at once. «They bought the bike outright» means they paid all the money at once, with no loan to pay back.
fact: Outright is an adjective in «an outright winner» and an adverb in «She told him outright».
fact: «Outright» can mean complete and total, as in «an outright lie».
fact: «Outright» can mean openly and directly, as in «He refused outright».
fact: To buy something outright means to pay the full price at once, with no loan.
fact: A good check is to swap in a word like total or openly and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «She won the race outright, ahead of everyone else.» What does outright mean here? | Only just, by a tiny margin | *Clearly and totally, with no doubt | Slowly and quietly | Late, after the others
exercise: «Sam told the teacher outright that he had broken the window.» What does outright mean? | *Openly and directly, with nothing hidden | Quietly and sadly | Almost, but not quite | Under the table, where no one could see
exercise: «Mum paid for the sofa outright, so there was no loan to pay back.» What does paying outright mean? | Paying part of the price now | Paying nothing at all | *Paying the full price at once, with no loan | Paying with a loan over many months
test: «It was an outright lie, and everyone knew it.» What does outright mean? | Very funny | Partly true | Quiet and hard to hear | *Total and complete, with no doubt
test: «Ben refused outright to share his sweets.» What does refused outright suggest? | *Said no openly and directly | Forgot to answer | Asked for more | Said yes slowly
exam: «Liam laughed outright when the clown fell over.» What does laughed outright mean? | Laughed a little, then stopped | *Laughed loudly and openly, without holding back | Did not laugh at all | Laughed quietly behind his hand
